# Captured run output under docs/<ticket>/logs/ is evidence: it is committed so that a
# measurement can be re-read later, and it must stay byte-identical to what the tool
# actually printed. Rewriting it to satisfy a whitespace check would falsify it. Turn the
# check off for those paths instead, so `git diff --check` stays useful everywhere else.
#
# `-whitespace` is not the whole check. `git diff --check` reports a leftover conflict
# marker independently of the whitespace attribute, and a pytest failure log quotes the
# source it failed on -- which is how a committed log came to contain a line of seven `<`
# and be reported as an unresolved merge. The lever for that half is the marker LENGTH:
# setting it far above the seven characters git looks for means no line in a log can be
# mistaken for one. Verified: with `-whitespace` alone a log containing `<<<<<<< HEAD` is
# still reported; with `conflict-marker-size` set it is not.
docs/**/logs/**       -whitespace conflict-marker-size=200
docs/**/*.log         -whitespace conflict-marker-size=200
docs/**/*-diff.txt    -whitespace conflict-marker-size=200
